Why a splash at all
===================

The default is a scrolling list of systemd units, which is a debugging tool
shown to everybody by default. It tells a person who can read it very little
they need, and a person who cannot read it that something is wrong.

`quiet splash` was already on the kernel command line. Without plymouth
installed, `splash` does nothing at all, which is why the messages were still
there -- the intent had been recorded and never implemented.

The blossom turns and there is no progress bar, because a progress bar claims
knowledge of how long a boot will take and nothing has that. When the disk is
encrypted plymouth also owns the passphrase prompt, so the first thing typed
on the machine is typed into something that looks like the machine.
